# What must not enter the build context.
#
# Two separate reasons, and the second is the one that matters. Excluding build
# output and caches keeps the context small. Excluding secrets keeps them out of
# the image entirely — a `COPY . .` puts every file in a layer, and a layer is
# readable by anyone who can pull the image, whether or not the final stage
# still references it.

# Installed dependencies. Copying the host's would also mean copying its native
# builds, which are compiled for the wrong platform and make the install inside
# the image inconsistent with its own lockfile.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output, regenerated inside the image.
dist
dist-node
**/dist
**/dist-node
.wrangler

# Secrets and local configuration. None of these belong in a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.dev.vars
**/.dev.vars.*
!**/.dev.vars.example
# Registry credentials: an .npmrc routinely carries an auth token, and the build
# uses the lockfile rather than a private registry.
**/.npmrc
# Keys and certificates, by extension rather than by name — the risk is the file
# somebody drops in while debugging TLS, which will not be called anything known.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.pfx
*.p12
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

# The maintainer's private braindump, ignored globally in git and never shipped.
initial-idea.md

# History and tooling that the build has no use for.
.git
.github
.claude
.remember
.codex
coverage
*.log
